\title{Per-Example Predictive Multiplicity in Hybrid Linear Recurrent Architectures}

\author{Anonymous}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Practitioners select among hybrid linear recurrent architectures using aggregate benchmarks, but these scores can mask which specific examples each architecture gets right. Across three controlled designs, adding attention affects substantially different examples per architecture (Jaccard $\approx$ 0.10, approximately twice the independence baseline); validation across five architectures and ten pairwise comparisons confirms this on all four benchmarks (all $p < .01$). Using the model zoo of \citet{wang2025hybrid} (three architectures, five attention ratios, two scales), we decompose per-example effects and find that this predictive multiplicity \citep{marx2020predictive} depends on task type ($\alpha = 0.14$--$0.30$) more than attention dose. Aggregate trends do not reach statistical significance ($n = 5$; Bonferroni-adjusted $p = .170$), yet per-example overlap saturates at 4\% attention and remains flat across all four benchmarks. Majority vote fails to exploit this complementarity, confirming that configurations interchangeable by aggregate metrics carry hidden per-example consequences. Confidence-informed routing extracts 36\% of oracle complementarity on LAMBADA ($+5.2$\,pp vs.\ majority vote), suggesting these differences are partially actionable.
\end{abstract}

\input{sections/introduction}

\input{sections/related_work}

\input{sections/method}

\input{sections/experiments}

\input{sections/conclusion}

\section*{Limitations}

\paragraph{Single training runs.} Each configuration corresponds to a single training run, so architectural and seed-specific effects cannot be fully disentangled. The $3\times$ within- vs.\ cross-architecture Jaccard gap and Pythia 70M seed calibration (within-seed $J$ is 2.7--4.3$\times$ higher; Appendix~\ref{app:seed_calibration}) provide proxy evidence, and consistency across all 40 architecture--benchmark combinations (all $p < .01$) would be unlikely under seed noise alone. With $n = 5$ attention ratios, the minimum achievable $p$ is 0.017, so LAMBADA's Bonferroni-adjusted $p = .170$ does not reach conventional significance.

\paragraph{Coverage.} We examine three primary architectures (plus two extended) from one model zoo, four main benchmarks, and two scale points. Generalization to Mamba, RWKV, other hybrid families, and generation tasks remains open. Results at larger scales (e.g., 7B+) may differ, particularly as attention mechanisms become more expressive with increased capacity.

\paragraph{Pre-training only.} All evaluations use pretrained checkpoints without fine-tuning; per-example effects may not persist after task-specific adaptation.

\section*{Code and Data Availability}

All models are from the publicly available model zoo of \citet{wang2025hybrid}. Our analysis code and per-example evaluation outputs will be released upon publication.

\bibliography{references}

\appendix
\input{sections/appendix}

\end{document}

